# ===========================================================================
# Unified deployment configuration. Copy to .env per site.
#
# The four tiers deploy to DIFFERENT HARDWARE. The values below are what makes a
# tier reachable from its neighbor — on one host they resolve to container
# networks; on real hardware, set them to the routed addresses from NETWORKING.md.
# ===========================================================================

# ---- inter-tier addressing (the only cross-hardware coupling) -------------
# Where the ENCLAVE's puller reaches the DMZ receiver (enclave --outbound--> DMZ).
# HTTPS is not optional. What crosses this connection is a memory image: every credential,
# key, token and open file the host had in RAM. The custody seal proves the bundle was not
# ALTERED; it does nothing to stop it being READ. Endpoints under suspicion are usually on
# segments the responder neither controls nor trusts.
RECEIVER_URL=https://receiver:8090
# Where the DMZ broker forwards analyst sessions (DMZ --> enclave ingress).
ENCLAVE_INGRESS=traefik:443
# The analyst-facing port. The DISTRIBUTOR binds this; it is the only port the tailnet ACL
# admits (hashicorp/access/policy.hujson) and the only way to a brokered session.
BROKER_LISTEN=8443
# Independent Boundary sessions, one per loopback port from here upward, reachable only by the
# distributor in the bastion's namespace. One shared session is a fleet-wide failure domain.
# These ports need no ACL, being unaddressable from outside that namespace.
# Egress workers carrying the sessions, in the enclave. Their number bounds the blast radius
# of a worker loss; it does not raise the connection-setup rate.
BOUNDARY_EGRESS_WORKERS=3
BROKER_SESSION_BASE=18443
BROKER_SESSIONS=8
# New analyst connections accepted per second at the distributor. The fragile hop is each
# session client's connection setup, so this does not scale with sessions OR workers: accepts
# are spread at a fixed, measured rate and established connections are unaffected. Empty
# means 8.
BROKER_ACCEPT_RATE=
# App-tier concurrency. Gunicorn sync workers each hold ONE in-flight request, so this count
# is the number of simultaneous requests the platform serves; everything beyond it queues.
# Size to (2 x cores) + 1. Empty means 17, sized for the 8-core reference host — three
# workers measured an error-rate knee at 30-50 concurrent analysts, under the 50 required.
IR_GUNICORN_WORKERS=

# ---- analyst access: WireGuard tunnel via headscale --------------------------
# The address EVERY tailnet node dials for the control plane, and the one its certificate must
# cover. Analyst workstations are separate machines and cannot address a container IP on the
# DMZ host, so a real deployment sets this to the DMZ's hostname or routable IP. The container
# name shipped as the default works only for single-host validation.
HEADSCALE_ADDR=headscale:8080
HEADSCALE_URL=http://headscale:8080
# Published on the DMZ host so off-host workstations can reach the control plane and STUN.
HEADSCALE_PORT=8080
HEADSCALE_STUN_PORT=3478
# Pre-auth keys are minted by deploy.sh from headscale and written to .env.tailnet — they are
# short-lived and per-node, so they are not committed here.
RECEIVER_PORT=8090
# The single public origin: app, IdP login and the SSO callback are all path-routed here,
# so exactly one port is brokered at the DMZ boundary.
PLATFORM_PUBLIC_URL=https://ir-platform.local:8443
KEYCLOAK_PUBLIC_URL=https://ir-platform.local:8443
OAUTH2_COOKIE_SECRET=VrzlIvvOwHJz6eqF7N5JjVI-DWbgrX_pHxlg8nAmiSs=

# ---- analyst workstations -------------------------------------------------
# Every workstation issued a tailnet identity. Each id becomes a node name, a compose project
# (its own containers and state volume) and its own pre-auth key. Two sharing an id are ONE
# node, and the tunnel then works only for whichever registered last.
#   deploy.sh workstation ws-002     # list the id here first, then deploy.sh dmz for its key
IR_WS_IDS=analyst

# ---- analyst-side network (fixed, so DNS + broker are stable) -------------
RUNTIME_DNS_ZONE=dns.podman
EDGE_SUBNET=10.89.30.0/24
DMZLINK_SUBNET=10.89.0.0/24
DNS_EDGE_IP=10.89.30.53
ENCLAVE_SUBNET=10.89.1.0/24
ENCLAVE_DNS_IP=10.89.1.53
# The tailnet control plane needs a FIXED address, not a name: tailscaled takes over
# /etc/resolv.conf when it starts, so a node cannot resolve its own control plane by
# container name — it then falls back to public DERP bootstrap, which the edge network
# deliberately cannot reach, and the node never logs in.
IR_PLATFORM_URL=https://ir-platform.local:8443/

# ---- mesh addressing ------------------------------------------------------
# The address each mesh service is REGISTERED at is stated here, never discovered. A discovered
# address is stale the moment the runtime recreates a container, and every stale registration
# surfaces far from its cause — as a proxy binding an address that no longer exists, or an
# application refused by a database that is healthy.
#
# Single host: the static container address below (inside ENCLAVE_SUBNET, outside the dynamic
# pool). To change one: edit it here, redeploy — deploy.sh validates the set before bringing
# anything up. Works on podman and docker alike; ipv4_address is standard compose.
#
# Multi-host: IR_MESH_ADDR_<SERVICE> is the ROUTABLE address of the host a service runs on and
# takes precedence over these. An enclave spanning hosts also sets IR_MESH_MULTIHOST=1 and
# IR_MESH_PORT_<SVC>. Traffic on those ports is Connect mTLS — reachability widens,
# authorization does not. Full explanation in deploy/NETWORKING.md.
#   IR_MESH_ADDR_IR_POSTGRES=10.20.30.11 · IR_MESH_MULTIHOST=1 · IR_MESH_PORT_DB=21001
IR_IP_DB=10.89.1.201
IR_IP_MINIO=10.89.1.202
IR_IP_VAULT=10.89.1.203
IR_IP_BACKEND=10.89.1.204
IR_IP_WORKER=10.89.1.205
IR_IP_FRONTEND=10.89.1.206
IR_IP_PULLER=10.89.1.207
IR_IP_REDIS=10.89.1.208
IR_IP_OAUTH2_PROXY=10.89.1.209
IR_IP_LOG_SHIPPER=10.89.1.210
IR_IP_KEYCLOAK=10.89.1.211

# Smartcard (CAC/PIV) logon. OFF unless a real PKI is present: it needs an issuing CA to
# trust, cards carrying its certificates, and revocation data staged into the enclave.
# See hashicorp/keycloak/pki-logon/README.md. Enabling it without trust anchors fails
# closed and locks every analyst out, so the deploy refuses that combination.
IR_PKI_LOGON=0

# ---- database -------------------------------------------------------------
POSTGRES_DB=ir_platform
# The static admin credential lives in .env.db (data tier only) — see that file.
POSTGRES_HOST=db
POSTGRES_PORT=5432

# ---- object store ---------------------------------------------------------
S3_ENDPOINT_URL=http://minio:9000
S3_ACCESS_KEY=ir_platform
S3_SECRET_KEY=CHANGE_ME
S3_BUCKET=ir-evidence
S3_REGION=us-east-1
S3_USE_TLS=0

# ---- app secrets (Vault supplies these in a hardened deployment) ----------
DJANGO_SECRET_KEY=CHANGE_ME
DJANGO_DEBUG=0
DJANGO_ALLOWED_HOSTS=*
IR_AUDIT_HMAC_KEY=CHANGE_ME
IR_CUSTODY_HMAC_KEY=CHANGE_ME
IR_BROKER_TOKEN=CHANGE_ME_40_CHARS
IR_SSO_PROXY_SECRET=CHANGE_ME

# ---- identity -------------------------------------------------------------
KEYCLOAK_ADMIN=admin
KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_PASSWORD=CHANGE_ME
# Dev/UAT only — the realm's per-role break-glass logins. Disable in production.
IR_SEED_DEMO_USERS=1
IR_ADMIN_PASSWORD=admin-demo-pw
IR_ANALYST_PASSWORD=analyst-demo-pw
IR_AUDITOR_PASSWORD=auditor-demo-pw

# ---- collection -----------------------------------------------------------
IR_INCIDENT_ID=INC-0001

# Carve ONLY regions with an indicator behind them. Set to 1 to carve every region a rule
# touches — a test aid for exercising the RE path, which buries the regions that matter among
# hundreds of benign ones.
IR_CARVE_ANY=0

# ---- reverse-engineering workstation --------------------------------------
# Which disassembler a session opens: binja or ghidra. Both are free of licensing, activation
# and call-home, so either runs with no network namespace — the containment is identical and
# only the tool differs. An installation standardises here so nobody has to remember a flag;
# `launch.sh --tool ghidra` overrides it for a single session.
IR_RE_TOOL=binja

# Ghidra only. A carved region is raw memory with no header, so the processor cannot be
# detected and has to be declared. This platform collects Linux x86-64.
IR_GHIDRA_PROCESSOR=x86:LE:64:default
# Run auto-analysis on import. An imported-but-unanalyzed region shows bytes rather than code,
# which is the first thing an analyst would fix by hand.
IR_GHIDRA_ANALYSIS=1

# ---- Boundary — the enclave access broker ---------------------------------
# The analyst's hop into the enclave is a Boundary SESSION: bound to a principal, checked against
# one named target, auditable. It replaced a socat forwarder used during initial bring-up, which
# moved bytes with no identity, no authorization and no record.
#
# Both Boundary processes run in the ENCLAVE. The controller holds the database, the grants and
# the encryption roots; the egress worker carries authorized sessions to the ingress and must
# therefore have a route to it. The DMZ runs a session CLIENT only, and no route of its own.
BOUNDARY_HOST=boundary
BOUNDARY_EGRESS_HOST=boundary-egress
BOUNDARY_DB_PASSWORD=CHANGE-ME
# The principal the bastion's session client authenticates as. A session that cannot be
# attributed to an identity is indistinguishable from an unauthenticated forwarder.
BOUNDARY_ANALYST_LOGIN=analyst
BOUNDARY_ANALYST_PASSWORD=CHANGE-ME

# The platform authenticates as this principal to SHOW brokered sessions. It can list and
# read sessions and nothing else — watching access must not become a way to obtain it.
BOUNDARY_SESSION_AUDITOR_LOGIN=session-auditor
BOUNDARY_SESSION_AUDITOR_PASSWORD=change-me-session-auditor

# Boundary's own encryption roots. RECOVERY authenticates provisioning with no account at all —
# deploy.sh uses it once; nothing else should.
#
# WORKER_AUTH is shared with the egress worker and is what registers it, with no approval step.
# That is acceptable only because both processes are in the enclave: a worker in an untrusted
# tier would have to register worker-led, so no key able to mint a worker ever sits there.
BOUNDARY_ROOT_KEY=CHANGE-ME
BOUNDARY_WORKER_AUTH_KEY=CHANGE-ME
BOUNDARY_RECOVERY_KEY=CHANGE-ME
# The enclave ingress Boundary brokers to — the SSO gate, and the only target that exists.
IR_ENCLAVE_INGRESS_HOST=traefik
IR_ENCLAVE_INGRESS_PORT=443

# ---- secrets ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Source app secrets from Vault (dynamic Postgres credentials + KV) rather than from this
# file. The values above are then the bootstrap admin credentials only — the application tier
# never sees them. Set to 0 to run without Vault.
IR_VAULT=1

# ---- service mesh (Consul Connect) ----------------------------------------
# IR_MESH=1 registers each service with Consul and runs an Envoy sidecar in front of it, so the
# intentions in hashicorp/consul/server.json are ENFORCED on every connection rather than only
# evaluated. Without it those intentions are a document nothing reads at connect time.
#
# The settings below ARE the enforcement. Binding Postgres and MinIO to loopback makes the
# sidecar the only listener the enclave network can reach; consumers then dial 127.0.0.1 on the
# same port number, so only the host changes. Set them to 0.0.0.0 / :9000 with IR_MESH=0 to run
# without the mesh — the platform works, and service authorization falls back to segmentation.
IR_MESH=1
IR_DB_LISTEN=127.0.0.1
IR_MINIO_LISTEN=127.0.0.1:9000
IR_REDIS_LISTEN=127.0.0.1
IR_DB_HOST_APP=127.0.0.1
IR_MINIO_ENDPOINT_APP=http://127.0.0.1:9000

# MULTI-HOST. The tiers deploy to separate hardware, and a sidecar is always co-located with the
# service it fronts — so what changes off a single host is only the address the rest of the mesh
# dials. Set one per service to that host's routable address; unset, the address is discovered
# from the container, which is what the single-host validation stack needs.
#   IR_MESH_ADDR_IR_POSTGRES=10.20.30.11
#   IR_MESH_ADDR_IR_MINIO=10.20.30.12
#   IR_MESH_ADDR_IR_BACKEND=10.20.30.13

# ---- analyst exports ------------------------------------------------------
# Where an IOC bundle or findings export written from the kiosk lands ON THIS HOST. The
# kiosk's filesystem is ephemeral, so without a host path the export is destroyed with the
# container. Left unset, deploy.sh resolves it per platform — the XDG download directory on
# Linux, ~/Downloads on macOS, the Windows user's Downloads under WSL or Git Bash — and
# creates it. Set it to pin one location, e.g. a case-specific evidence share.
#IR_EXPORT_DIR=
